  "title": "Relationship and Messaging Scams",
  "content": "Relationship scams use a fabricated or manipulated personal connection to obtain money, information, access, or influence. A romance scam is one form, but the same pattern can imitate friendship, mentorship, professional networking, or membership in a trusted community. The relationship is built as part of the attack rather than appearing only as decoration around a link.\n\n<b>Trust is accumulated slowly</b>\n\nThe attacker may maintain ordinary conversation for days or weeks, mirror the target's interests, offer emotional support, and avoid obvious demands. Later requests often begin small: keep a secret, verify a detail, forward a message, open a file, connect an account, or help with access. Compliance with a small request can make the next one feel consistent with an already trusted relationship.\n\nInstant-messaging spam can exploit the same trust more quickly by arriving from a compromised account or group chat. A familiar display name, shared group, or believable conversation history does not guarantee that the current sender controls the account safely. Sudden changes in tone, new secrecy, requests to move platforms, unexpected financial trouble, or requests involving credentials and workplace access deserve independent verification.\n\n<b>Verify without shaming</b>\n\nConfirm sensitive requests through another known channel and check identity before sharing access or private information. Do not confront the suspected scammer if doing so could increase personal risk; preserve the account, timeline, messages, payment details, links, and requested actions, then report them through the appropriate service and organizational channel.\n\nSomeone who trusted a fabricated relationship was deliberately manipulated, not foolish. Supportive reporting protects evidence and makes it more likely that other affected people will come forward.",
  "links": [
    "relationship built",
    "message timeline",
    "message history"
  ]
}
